 "result": "maze.py is built and verified: it reads the grid from stdin and prints the shortest 4-neighbour path length from S to E, or -1, and runs in about 0.03 s on the 2000x2000 maze (gen.py seed 3, answer 4078). It has three tiers that return the same answer: a C extension BFS, a ctypes-loaded C BFS, and a pure-Python BFS fallback. Evidence: fuzz.py ran 5980 checks across all tiers against an independent reference BFS (395 unreachable cases, including ragged rows, CRLF and no trailing newline) with 0 mismatches; the big maze gives 4078 from every tier, from ref2.py (independent pure-Python BFS) and from big.ans; s.txt gives 122 and t.txt gives -1, both matching ref.py. On speed, bench.py (10 runs on the big maze) measures about 27 runs/s for the current body against 12.4\u201312.8 for the previous ctypes-only version. That roughly 2.1x gain was made by direct edit after the assay was registered, not through a scored `try`, so the registered baseline of 12.41 reflects the old body and the gain is my own measurement rather than a harness-confirmed win. Python startup (~0.02 s) now dominates the run time, so further gains would be small.",
